Hash: SHA512

annotations:
  artifacthub.io/changes: |
    - kind: changed
      description: Much more tolerant readinessProbe/livenessProbe initialDelaySeconds (10s->30s / 30s->90s) and a more realistic resources.requests.cpu (500m->1) - under real CPU contention on a shared GPU node, the old tight probe timing was killing containers mid-boot before the KasmVNC desktop finished starting, causing a self-sustaining restart storm
  artifacthub.io/changesHistory: |
    - version: 1.4.1
      changes:
        - kind: fixed
          description: "sunshine.service.externalTrafficPolicy default changed from Local to Cluster - MetalLB's IP-sharing feature (allow-shared-ip) requires every Service sharing an external IP to have an IDENTICAL pod selector when using Local traffic policy, which is fundamentally incompatible with sharing one IP across multiple different emulator Deployments (confirmed in production, 2026-08-23: every non-first Service failed allocation with \"can't change sharing key\", regardless of annotation/port config). Cluster does not have that restriction. Tradeoff: an extra kube-proxy hop and loss of the real client source IP, both minor for this use case."
    - version: 1.4.0
      changes: |
        - kind: changed
          description: "sunshine.hostNetwork now defaults to false, replaced by a new sunshine.service (LoadBalancer via MetalLB) as the default way to expose Moonlight's TCP/UDP ports. Root-caused in production (2026-08-23): with multiple sunshine.hostNetwork pods sharing one node, the base image's KasmVNC nginx (hardcoded on 3000/3001) collided across every pod but the first, permanently breaking browser access for all the others - LoadBalancer keeps each pod's own network namespace, so the collision can't happen regardless of how many sunshine-enabled emulators share a node. hostNetwork is kept as an opt-in fallback (sunshine.hostNetwork: true). sunshine.service supports MetalLB's allow-shared-ip (sunshine.service.sharedIPKey) so multiple emulator releases can share one external IP - set the same loadBalancerIP + sharedIPKey on each, with non-overlapping sunshine.port values."
    - version: 1.3.0
      changes: |
        - kind: added
          description: "probes.enabled (default true) - set to false to disable the readiness/liveness probe and the http/https containerPort declarations. Needed when running multiple sunshine.hostNetwork pods on the same node: the Selkies nginx (3000/3001) is hardcoded and can't be moved, so any pod beyond the first would otherwise crashloop or block scheduling entirely even though Sunshine itself works fine. Tradeoff: the web UI (KasmVNC) becomes unreachable on that pod - Moonlight is unaffected"
    - version: 1.2.1
      changes:
        - kind: fixed
          description: "sunshine.* now also mounts /dev/uhid - without it, Sunshine falls back to emulating gamepads as a generic Xbox One controller when the client reports a PlayStation-type pad (DualShock/DualSense), and that fallback does not correctly forward face buttons/triggers (confirmed: analog stick axes work, buttons don't) - the real virtual DualShock/DualSense device needs kernel uhid access to be created"
    - version: 1.2.0
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: sunshine.* (POC) - Sunshine/Moonlight low-latency game streaming as an alternative to the default KasmVNC streaming, requiring a Docker Mod (ghcr.io/henriqzimer/xemu-sunshine-mod) that installs Sunshine and swaps the base image's Xvfb for a real Xorg + dummy driver (Xvfb isn't linked against libudev so it can't hotplug Sunshine's uinput-created input devices), hostNetwork/privileged, and PIXELFLUX_WAYLAND=false in env
    - version: 1.1.5
      changes:
        - kind: fixed
          description: README install commands now use a unique repo alias (<name>-helm-chart) instead of the bare chart name, and pin --version explicitly, matching what actually works
    - version: 1.1.4
      changes:
        - kind: changed
          description: README now shows the emulator's logo at the top (linuxserver.io's own image, same one used for the chart icon)
    - version: 1.1.3
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: Chart releases are now signed (helm package --sign) - see artifacthub.io/signKey for the public key location and fingerprint, powers the "Signed" badge on ArtifactHub
    - version: 1.1.2
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: values.schema.json - validates values.yaml against a JSON Schema, and is what powers the "Values schema" feature/badge on ArtifactHub (previously absent)
    - version: 1.1.1
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: icon, previously missing entirely - pointed at linuxserver.io's own logo image for this app, which is why no image was ever showing on ArtifactHub
    - version: 1.1.0
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: gpu.vendor "nvidia" mode (alongside the existing "intel-amd" VA-API mode), requesting nvidia.com/gpu via the NVIDIA Kubernetes device plugin and setting NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES/NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES. Documented the nvidia-drm.modeset=1 kernel parameter (and, on headless nodes, a dummy HDMI/DP plug) needed on the node for the compositor to render anything instead of a solid black desktop
        - kind: added
          description: seccompUnconfined to work around a JIT recompiler crashing with SIGBUS under the default seccomp profile on some kernel/libseccomp combinations
        - kind: changed
          description: Bumped the default shmSize from 1Gi to 2Gi, matching the fix confirmed on this chart's sibling pcsx2-helm-chart for a JIT recompiler crashing with SIGBUS under Docker/Kubernetes' small default /dev/shm
    - version: 1.0.0
      changes:
        - kind: added
          description: Initial release of the xemu Helm chart
